P1: two real worker processes; one is lost, the other is not affected
  both workers started (fork + exec) and reported their cs7 instance
  with >300 descriptors (one above 1023) open in the main process, each worker holds only stdio and its IPC socket
  worker 0 was taken down: SIGTERM ignored, SIGKILL after the grace period
  its replacement started only after the old process was collected
  worker 1 stayed READY with the same process, and its event loop kept running
  the main event loop was never blocked while worker 0 was taken down
  the restarted worker 0 also holds only stdio and its IPC socket
  a process that cannot be collected keeps its worker TERMINATING and is reported as stuck; no second generation is started, worker 1 is unaffected
  once it can be collected, the worker is restarted
  stopping the pool collects every worker (the one ignoring SIGTERM via SIGKILL): no process, no zombie left
P2: the main process is SIGKILLed; its workers do not survive it
  both workers were terminated by the parent-death signal (SIGTERM) and collected
P3: the main process dies between fork() and PR_SET_PDEATHSIG
  the child noticed it was orphaned and exited before exec: no worker without its main process
P4: the executable file is replaced while the main process runs
  a worker restarted after its executable was deleted and replaced still runs the original image
P5: osmo-bsc shuts down; GOODBYE reaches every worker, which exits 0 by itself
  each worker received GOODBYE although the event loop had already ended, and exited 0: no EOF, no SIGTERM
  aworker_pool_stop() returned well within the grace period
P6: the workers take a while to exit after GOODBYE
  aworker_pool_stop() waited for them to exit by themselves (no SIGTERM), well within the grace period
P7: a worker restart is due when the pool is stopped; no replacement is started
  worker 0 was RESTARTING with its restart overdue; worker 1 READY, so stop polled the event loop
  a timer due at the same time as the restart fired while aworker_pool_stop() ran
  yet aworker_pool_stop() started no process
P8: a worker that cannot use its config file is FAILED; the others and osmo-bsc go on
  worker 1 exited 66 and is FAILED (reason config, with the file and the time); worker 0 READY
  it stays FAILED past restart-delay and startup-timeout: no process started
  resetting a worker that is not FAILED is refused and changes nothing
  after the file is fixed, a reset starts exactly that worker once; it becomes READY
  worker 0 kept its process throughout, and osmo-bsc was never asked to shut down
P9: a worker claiming a cs7 instance its file did not configure at start is FAILED
  worker 1 reported instance 2, its file configured 1: FAILED (reason ownership); worker 0 READY
  it stays FAILED past restart-delay and startup-timeout: no process started
  once it reports what its file configured, a reset starts it once and it becomes READY
  worker 0 kept its process throughout, and osmo-bsc was never asked to shut down
P10: a worker lost again and again is restarted with an exponential backoff, capped
  the first restart waits restart-delay (5 s), each further loss doubles it: 5, 10, 20, 40 s
  restart-delay-max (60 s) caps it: 60, 60 s; no start 1 ms before a delay, exactly one at it
  worker 0 kept its process and stayed READY, and osmo-bsc was never asked to shut down
P11: a worker that was READY for stable-time before it was lost starts the backoff over
  READY 59.999 s, then lost: the delay still doubles (5 -> 10 s)
  READY 60 s (stable-time), then lost: back to restart-delay (5 s); lost again at once: 10 s
  the reported backoff step drops to 0 exactly when the worker has been READY for stable-time
P12: time spent starting is not stable time: a startup timeout continues the backoff
  a process that never became READY ran 65 s (more than stable-time) until the startup timeout:
  that loss is the third in a row (5, 10, 20 s), not a fresh start
P13: a worker lost again and again is FAILED once its restarts reach 'max-restarts 5 window 300'
  4 restarts (5, 10, 20, 40 s); the loss that would be the 5th restart within 300 s makes it FAILED
  (reason restart-limit; the detail names the restarts, the window and the limit)
  it stays FAILED: no process started in the 200 s that follow (the startup timer fires meanwhile)
  a reset starts it once with the record and the backoff forgotten: lost again, restarted after 5 s
  worker 0 kept its process and stayed READY, and osmo-bsc was never asked to shut down
P14: the restart window is per worker and slides: a restart counts while it is less than S s old
  'max-restarts 2 window 300': worker 0, restarted at 0 s, lost again at 299.999 s: FAILED
  at 300 s its status no longer counts that restart
  worker 1, restarted at 0 s too, lost again at 300 s: that restart left the window, it is restarted
P15: a planned stop never counts as an unplanned loss
  'max-restarts 1': a worker lost while the pool runs is FAILED (restart-limit) at once
  the same loss handled while the pool stops: not counted, not FAILED, no restart, nothing started
P16: stable-time resets the backoff, not the restart record
  lost at 0, 5, 15 s (5, 10, 20 s), then READY 60 s and lost: restarted after restart-delay (5 s)
  the restarts of 0, 5 and 15 s still count, and that restart is added: 4 within 300 s
  READY 60 s again, lost at 160 s: restart 5 within 300 s, so FAILED (restart-limit)
P17: each start has its own startup timeout ('startup-timeout 60')
  worker 0 started at 5 s, worker 1 at 25 s, neither READY: at 64.999 s both still starting;
  at 65 s worker 0 is timed out, worker 1 keeps its process
  the timeout is one more unplanned loss: second restart in the window, backoff 10 s
  worker 0 restarted after 10 s and became READY
  worker 1 is timed out at 85 s, its own deadline, not before; worker 0 stays READY with its process
  the READY worker 0 is never timed out later (200 s: same process, no further loss)
P18: a worker that becomes READY just before its startup deadline is not timed out
  started at 5 s, READY at 64.999 s
  at 65 s and up to 300 s: READY with the same process, no loss
P19: a startup timeout never outlives its process
  process started at 5 s lost at 30 s; its replacement, started at 40 s, is not timed out at 65 s
  that one lost at 99 s: its deadline (100 s) passes while the worker waits, without effect;
  the next process starts at 119 s as scheduled
  which is timed out at 179 s, its own deadline: fourth restart in the window, backoff 40 s
  stopping osmo-bsc while a process is starting leaves no startup timer behind
P20: startup-timeout is 60 s by default; a configured value replaces it
  default: a process started at 5 s is still starting at 64.999 s, timed out at 65 s
  'startup-timeout 30': still starting at 34.999 s, timed out at 35 s
P21: workers of the inline form start from their sealed memfd snapshots
  each worker's command line is --aworker-fd, --aworker-index and --aworker-snapshot-fd (no --aworker-config)
  with >300 descriptors open in the main process, each worker held exactly stdio, its IPC socket and memfd:bsc-aworker-<N>
  each read exactly its own worker view and closed the descriptor before going on
  the main process holds the two snapshots, close-on-exec
  stopping the pool closes them; no process left
P22: a worker cannot reach another worker's snapshot, nor change any snapshot
  worker 0 held and read only memfd:bsc-aworker-0 (cs7 instance 0), worker 1 only memfd:bsc-aworker-1 (cs7 instance 1)
  through /proc/<main pid>/fd each worker can open both snapshots (same user) and read them, but writing and truncating fail with EPERM (sealed)
P23: the main configuration file edited, replaced or deleted: a restarted worker reads the original
  edited in place: the restarted worker 1 read its view as of the start
  replaced (rename over it): the same
  deleted: the same
  every restart used the one snapshot made at startup (same descriptor, still two)
P24: restarts read the whole snapshot, whatever its shared file offset
  with the offset at the end, two restarts each read the complete view; the offset is unchanged
P25: a worker reset after FAILED starts from the snapshot made at startup
  a worker that cannot use its snapshot is FAILED (config), naming the snapshot
  reset: it read its view as of the start, not the edited file, and the log says so
P26: a worker of the inline form claiming another worker's cs7 instance is FAILED (ownership)
  worker 1 reported cs7 instance 0: FAILED (ownership); worker 0 READY
P27: the main process' snapshot descriptors: restart, stop, fork failure, exec failure
  two snapshots while running; a restart neither adds nor replaces one
  stop: none left
  fork failure (RLIMIT_NPROC 0): the start fails, no process, no snapshot left
  exec failure: both workers FAILED (config), the two snapshots kept for a reset
  stop: none left, no process left
P28: no sealed memfd and no usable fallback directory: the start is refused before any fork
  $RUNTIME_DIRECTORY a symbolic link, /dev/shm not a directory, /tmp missing (all redirected): refused, no fork, no snapshot, nothing left in any directory
P29: the fallback snapshot: delivered, re-checked before every start, refused once changed
  each worker got its unlinked, read-only fallback file (WARNING logged); no directory left behind
  unchanged: a restart is allowed and reads it again
  changed through /proc/<pid>/fd after fchmod: the restart is refused before fork, worker 1 FAILED (config); worker 0 READY
  a reset is refused the same way
P30: a pool configuration that differs from the file read at startup is refused before any fork
  worker 1 on another cs7 instance: refused
  another number of workers: refused
  no fork, no pool, no snapshot
  the same configuration as the file: started
  the file read at startup uses the file form, the running configuration the inline form: refused
PASSED
